window.docContents[29].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Compact-carrier-smooth-transport":{"contents":"If , where  is a finite-dimensional real normed\nspace,  is compact, and  is open,\nthen there is a test function  such thatand  on a neighborhood of every point of .\n\nChoose a compact set  with  in its interior and .\nMathlib's smooth separation theorem gives a smooth function with values in\n that equals one on a neighborhood of  and vanishes outside .\nIts support lies in the compact set , so it is a test function\nwith the stated properties.\n\nThe cutoff compensates for the failure of properness. When  is not proper,\nthe composite  generally loses compact support. Multiplying by\na fixed test function  restores it at the price of a dependence on\n.\n\nLet  be an arbitrary smooth map between real normed spaces and let\n be fixed. Localized precomposition is the continuous\nlinear mapHere  need not be proper.\n\nThe output support is controlled by  alone rather than by\n, and this uniform support bound is what\nmakes the seminorm estimates behind continuity possible without properness.\n\nFor every test function ,\n\nMultiplication by  fixes the output support inside one compact set.\nOn every compact input stage, Mathlib's iterated product and composition\nestimates bound the output seminorms by finitely many input seminorms.\nThese seminorm bounds prove continuity on each stage. The inductive-limit\ntopology then gives continuity on the full test-function space.\n\nTransposition again produces an operator on distributions, this time for\narbitrary smooth .\n\nTransposing localized test-function pullback definesfor every smooth map  and fixed cutoff .\n\nThe pairing formula differs from the proper case only by the factor \ninside the test function. When  equals one near a carrier of ,\nchanging  alters the test function only where  cannot see it.\n\nThe localized pushforward acts by\n\nThe operator is the compact-convergence transpose of\n. Evaluating the transpose on a test function gives the displayed\npairing formula directly.\n\nCutoff independence makes the pushforward of a compactly carried tensor product\nalong addition canonical, and this is how convolution is defined.\n\nSuppose  vanishes outside a compact set . Then\n vanishes outside , andIt therefore has a compact carrier. Moreover, if both  and \nequal one near , thenThus compactly carried distributions have a cutoff-independent pushforward\nalong arbitrary smooth maps.\n\nA target test function supported outside  vanishes on a neighborhood\nof . Its pullback therefore vanishes on a neighborhood of , as\ndoes its product with the cutoff.\nThe localized action formula therefore transports the carrier to ,\nwhich is compact by continuity of . For cutoff independence, the\ndifference  vanishes on a neighborhood of\n, so the carrier hypothesis makes its pairing with  zero.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.9. Compact-carrier smooth transport","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Compact-carrier-smooth-transport"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Cox___de-Boor-B-splines":{"contents":"B-splines are the standard basis for certified piecewise-polynomial\napproximation. A collocation matrix with a checked positive determinant\ncertifies that a finite family is linearly independent. Tensor products of these one-dimensional\nfamilies provide profile representations on rectangles.\n\nA knot sequence is a nondecreasing mapbundled as an order homomorphism. Repeated knots are allowed, and a finite\nknot vector is represented by repeating its final knot with the desired\nendpoint multiplicity.\n\nMonotonicity of the knots is the only structure the recursion needs. The\nblending weights divide by the knot differences  and\n, which are nonnegative because the sequence is\nnondecreasing and may vanish when knots repeat.\n\nThe degree-zero B-spline with index  is the indicator of the half-open\nknot cell , and the recursiondefines the higher degrees, with the convention that a quotient with zero\ndenominator equals zero. That convention is Lean's totalized division, and it\nis exactly the standard treatment of repeated knots. A finite family selects\nthe indices  of a fixed degree.\n\nWith knots , , and , one step of the recursion\nproduces the familiar degree-one hat:\n\n\n\nOnly the two adjacent cells contribute. In a linear combination of such\nbasis functions, changing one coefficient changes the function only near\nthat coefficient's knot interval. The support theorem states this\nlocality. Whether specified sample values determine the coefficients\nuniquely is a different question, answered by the collocation theorem.\n\nThe recursion combines  and  with weights that are\nnonnegative on the supports  and  of\nthose two children, and both supports lie inside .\nInduction on the degree therefore propagates support and sign information\nfrom the degree-zero indicators, and the strict positivity statement is what\nthe Schoenberg–Whitney diagonal condition later relies on.\n\nFor every nondecreasing knot sequence, degree , and index ,so the B-spline vanishes for  and for . Moreover\nit is strictly positive on the open interior of its canonical support,including when interior knots are repeated.\n\nAll statements are proved by induction on the degree. At degree zero they\nare properties of the indicator of a half-open cell. In the recursion, a\nnonzero value requires one of the two child B-splines to be nonzero, and the\nchild supports  and  lie inside\n. On those supports both blending weights are\nnonnegative, giving nonnegativity. For strict positivity, away from a\nrepeated knot block one of the two children is positive by induction and its\nweight is positive; when the point coincides with an entire block of equal\nknots, the left-clamped identity  resolves the right-continuous\nconvention.\n\nNonnegativity and local support describe individual B-splines; linear\nindependence of a finite family is a statement about all of them at once,\nand the cleanest certificate for it is a matrix. Evaluating  functions at\n points gives a square matrix whose nonsingularity forces any vanishing\nlinear combination to have zero coefficients, so independence is transferred\nto the question of a nonzero determinant.\n\nFor a finite family of functions  and matching points\n, indexed by the same finite type, the collocation matrix isIf  is a field and , the family  is linearly\nindependent as -valued functions: a vanishing combination evaluated\nat the points gives a vanishing product with , and invertibility forces\nthe coefficients to vanish.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009B-Splines and Schoenberg–Whitney Collocation","header":"9.2.1.10.1. Cox–de Boor B-splines","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Cox___de-Boor-B-splines"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--Domains___-Types___-and-Side-Conditions":{"contents":"The calculator frontend has fixed real semantics. Writing\n(x : ℝ) documents that semantics; it does not select a different backend.\nA complex annotation is rejected.\n\nInside ordinary theorem goals, coefficients can come from many Lean types as\nlong as the resulting expression is real-valued. Natural, integer, rational,\nand nonnegative-real casts, structure fields, and arbitrary real-valued\nparameters may remain opaque coefficients. Differentiated coordinates\nthemselves are real.\n\nEvaluation is total because Lean's real division, logarithm, square root, and\nreal power are total functions. Classical calculus rules are not\nunconditional. The predicate Expr.ValidAt ρ e records the facts needed at a\npoint: denominators and logarithm arguments are nonzero, square-root arguments\nand the bases of real powers are strictly positive, and the argument of an\ninverse sine or cosine differs from . The analytic logarithm\nderivative bridge therefore needs  (or a stronger hypothesis such as\n), and the power rule for  needs . This is distinct\nfrom identities and enclosures about the total value, which need not assume\nanalytic validity.\n\nFor example,\n holds when . At , Lean's\ntotalized division makes the left side zero, whereas the right side is two.\nThe side condition protects the equality itself. For an interval calculation,\na bound such as  also controls division quantitatively:\nthe reciprocal has magnitude at most . Domain information\ntherefore affects both the validity of an exact simplification and the width\nof a numerical certificate.\n\nEvery checked frontend, whether it normalizes, differentiates, integrates, or\nlowers a PDE residual, generates the same small family of obligations:\nnonvanishing denominators and logarithm arguments, positive square-root\narguments and power bases, and finite lists of such facts collected as\nValidAt, ValidityRequirements.Holds, or Alg.SideHolds. One tactic,\nsymbolic_assumptions, discharges all of them. It first reduces the\nrequirement container to a conjunction of atomic real facts without rewriting\nthe atoms, so a generated condition keeps the shape a user hint refers to, and\nthen closes each atom by exactly one of assumption, positivity,\nnorm_num, or nlinarith, also in their ≠ 0 forms; a disequality\nhypothesis  closes , and a positive hypothesis\n also discharges a nonvanishing condition . Evidence given through a\nusing clause is simply added to the local context first. On failure the\ntactic names the atoms it could not prove and leaves the goal unchanged; no\nassumption is ever inserted.\n\nThese obligations explain why a plausible simplification can stop short of a\nproof. Cancelling a denominator requires evidence that it is nonzero; if we\ncannot establish that condition, we still have work to do. The question of\nhow a calculator should expose such conditions also appears in a 2020 Lean\nZulip wishlist of computer-algebra features\n(Effectiveness as a computer algebra system?)\nand again in 2023\n(Is there a field_simp variant that generates hypotheses?).\nHere the obligations travel with the result. The tactic must prove them from\nthe available context, including any supplied hints, before it can use the\ncalculation to close the goal.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Proof-producing computation","header":"10.4.3. Domains, Types, and Side Conditions","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--Domains___-Types___-and-Side-Conditions"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--From-a-Calculation-to-a-Theorem":{"contents":"Lowercase cas is a proof tactic. It works against the ordinary proposition\nalready written by the user. For example, a theorem with conclusion\n can be closed by cas. A logarithm\nexample with a hypothesis  uses cas using hx.\n\nThe derivative route accepts the ordinary Mathlib shapes in which such\nstatements are written. For an explicit function of one real variable these\nare deriv f x₀ = rhs, HasDerivAt f f' x₀, HasDerivWithinAt f f' s x₀,\nand nested derivatives deriv (fun x ↦ deriv g x) x₀, with the inner\nderivative applied or not. The inner rewrite uses a proved function equality,\nincluding for the exponential representations of sinh and cosh, and\nrequires its analytic conditions to hold at every real point.\nFor an explicit function on ℝ^n with a literal\ndimension n they are partialDeriv i f p = rhs, repeated partialDeriv,\nlaplacian f p = rhs, and fderiv ℝ f p (EuclideanSpace.single i 1) = rhs.\nThe same reflection certifies regularity: DifferentiableAt ℝ f x₀,\nDifferentiable ℝ f, ContDiffAt ℝ n f x₀, ContDiffOn ℝ n f s, and\nContDiff ℝ n f, on ℝ or on ℝ^k with literal k, for every smoothness\norder n, whether a natural literal, ⊤, a variable, or the analytic order,\nbecause the Mathlib lemmas for the elementary functions hold for every order.\nThe domain conditions are those of the derivative rules, at the point for the\nAt forms and at every point of the set or the space for the others. Bare\ncas dispatches on all of these shapes, and on interval integrals when the\nintegration frontend is imported; cas? closes the goal the same way and\nsuggests the explicit form.\n\nopen PDE (partialDeriv laplacian)\n\n-- A HasDerivAt statement about an\n-- exponential decay mode.\nexample (lambda t : ℝ) :\n    HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ ↦ Real.exp (-lambda * s))\n      (-lambda * Real.exp (-lambda * t)) t := by\n  cas\n\n-- A coordinate partial derivative on ℝ^2.\nexample (p : ℝ^2) :\n    partialDeriv 1 (fun q : ℝ^2 ↦ q 0 * Real.cos (q 1)) p =\n      -p 0 * Real.sin (p 1) := by\n  cas\n\n-- The coordinate Laplacian of a harmonic function.\nexample (p : ℝ^2) :\n    laplacian\n        (fun q : ℝ^2 ↦ Real.exp (q 0) * Real.sin (q 1)) p =\n      0 := by\n  cas\n\n-- A nested derivative is eliminated inner-first.\nexample (a x : ℝ) :\n    deriv (fun x ↦ deriv (fun y ↦ Real.sin (a * y)) x) x =\n      -(a ^ 2) * Real.sin (a * x) := by\n  cas\n\n-- The power rule with a real exponent needs\n-- the positivity of the base.\nexample (x : ℝ) (hx : 0 < x) :\n    deriv (fun t : ℝ ↦ t ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ)) x =\n      (3 / 2 : ℝ) * x ^ (1 / 2 : ℝ) := by\n  cas using hx\n\n-- A regularity statement on ℝ^2; the quotient's\n-- condition is read from the context.\nexample (p : ℝ^2) (hp : p 0 ≠ 0) :\n    DifferentiableAt ℝ (fun y : ℝ^2 ↦ y 1 / y 0) p := by\n  cas\n\n-- Smoothness of every order on a half-line.\nexample :\n    ContDiffOn ℝ (⊤ : ℕ∞) (fun t : ℝ ↦ 1 / t)\n      (Set.Ioi 0) := by\n  cas\n\n\nDefinitions of the current module that depend on the differentiated variable\nare unfolded automatically; an imported definition is opened with an explicit\nunfold so that the reader sees which definition is used, and a hypothesis\nstated through such a definition must be unfolded by hand before the analytic\nconditions can be matched against it. A false derivative value, a nested\nlogarithm that is not valid everywhere, or a differentiability claim whose\nanalytic condition is missing is refused without changing the proof state,\nand the error names the unproved condition. Outside the route are abstract\nfunctions, symbolic dimension (refused with a message asking for a literal\none), norms and inner products, sums, piecewise definitions, improper\nintegrals outside the supported half-line families, and vector-valued\nderivatives; those keep their Mathlib proofs.\n\nAn equality containing integrals or derivatives may need only algebra on\ntheir values. Bare cas first tries that algebra with the integral and\nderivative terms left as atoms. Moving an integral past another summand uses\ncommutativity of addition, even for an abstract integrand taking values in a\nnormed real vector space. Likewise, associating a sum containing deriv f a\ndoes not require a formula for the derivative.\n\nFor equalities, disequalities, and order relations that need explicit\nderivative values, the derivative-rewriting route rewrites every maximal\nderivative subterm of an explicit elementary function (deriv,\nnested deriv, partialDeriv, repeated partialDeriv, laplacian, the\ncoordinate fderiv) to its checked closed form, all or nothing, so that a\nresidual with several derivative terms becomes an ordinary identity. It then\nprepares the goal with theorem-backed steps only: a hypothesis \nyields both factor conditions, absolute values of sign-known arguments are\nevaluated, powers are distributed over products, the identities\n and  join the context wherever\n is provable, and constants such as  and  count as\npositive. Next come the checked simplifier with the local context and one\nMathlib arithmetic procedure, with the real-valued definitions of the current\nmodule unfolded on a retry. If that fails on a real identity, it reflects both\nsides over atoms, runs the side-conditioned normalizer in the kernel,\ncertifies the zero difference with decide +kernel, and discharges the\nrecorded conditions from the context or from using evidence. This is how\nresidual identities and the real-power and exponential identities close:\n\n-- The wave residual of sin (x - t): both\n-- derivatives are rewritten first.\nexample (t x : ℝ) :\n    deriv (deriv (fun s : ℝ ↦ Real.sin (x - s))) t\n        - deriv (deriv (fun y : ℝ ↦ Real.sin (y - t))) x\n      = 0 := by\n  cas\n\n-- π is a positive constant.\nexample : 4 * Real.pi * (1 / (4 * Real.pi)) = 1 := by\n  cas\n\n-- Powers of one base collect under 0 < a.\nexample (a s t : ℝ) (ha : 0 < a) :\n    a ^ (s / 2) * a ^ ((t - s) / 2) = a ^ (t / 2) := by\n  cas\n\n-- Exponentials recombine with no hypothesis.\nexample (u : ℝ) :\n    Real.exp u ^ 2 * Real.exp (-2 * u) = 1 := by\n  cas\n\n\nEquations in the context can also supply the algebraic relations used by\ncas. The explicit form cas using [h₁, h₂] adds separate equation proofs;\neach entry can be an applied theorem, such as h₁ 0. Over any commutative\nring, the equations a * d = b * c and c * f = e * d imply\nc * (a * f - b * e) = 0. The factor c matters: the equations alone do\nnot justify cancelling it.\n\nFor integrals, cas can also apply a direct theorem without\nselecting a primitive. Splitting the integral of a sum uses interval\nintegrability of both summands, for the actual measure and codomain.\nDifferentiating an integral with a varying endpoint uses the continuity,\nmeasurability and integrability premises of the corresponding fundamental\ntheorem; the left endpoint contributes a minus sign. These rules retain\nabstract functions and an abstract normed codomain in the statement.\nSupported real expressions with Lebesgue measure go through the scalar\ncertificate route.\n\ncas_nf constructs a checked equality for the expression it transforms and\nuses congruence to replace it in its surrounding context. An opaque predicate\ncan therefore retain its role in the statement while an algebraic argument\nis normalized. For example, suppose h asserts\n and hden proves .\nThe factorization  permits cancellation,\nso cas_nf using hden at h changes the hypothesis to .\nThe calculation needs no information about . If this is the desired\nconclusion, exact h completes the proof.\n\nBy default the tactic works in the goal; locations select hypotheses, and\nconv selects an occurrence inside a larger expression. A second copy can\nremain unchanged when only the first is needed for the next proof step.\nDomain requirements still belong to each transformed expression. In\nparticular, total division at zero does not justify cancelling a denominator,\nand  becomes  without a sign assumption.\n\nThe ordinary interface reflects real arithmetic over opaque atoms and\ntransports its checked equality back into the statement. Existing\nVerified.Expr.eval occurrences keep their evaluator and original index type.\nBoth interfaces use the established normalization pipeline; the resulting\nform need not be the shortest equivalent expression.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.9. From a Calculation to a Theorem","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--From-a-Calculation-to-a-Theorem"}});